From: mcmc-diagnostician (red-team marginal-k lane)
Subject: [PROPOSED, NOT APPLIED] fix(marginal-k): slice sampler must use the
 marginal-over-k likelihood, not fixed-kPrime; invalidate charLL cache

MARGINAL-K-SLICE-001
====================

Root cause: under likelihoodMode="marginal_k", both eval_slice_target() and the
acceptance recompute in slice_scalar_impl() fall through to cpp_log_likelihood()
(the fixed-kPrime / sampled-k evaluator using state->kPrime, which is pinned to
kObs in marginal-k mode). They have NO data->marginalK branch. Consequences:

 1. The slice target is the wrong density (fixed-k, not the marginal sum over k),
    so slice_rate_log_sd targets the sampled-k posterior.
 2. On acceptance, state->logLik is overwritten with the fixed-k value, which is
    higher than the marginal-over-k value by a constant offset (empirically +9.68
    on the 16-tip/100-char repro; see findings note exp14). This inflated baseline
    then makes every subsequent MH move (tree_length=0, beta_simplex=4,
    dirichlet=23/24, joint_tl_rls=21) reject deterministically, because their
    proposed marginal logLik is ~9.7 below the inflated baseline.
 3. charLLCacheReady is left untouched by the slice path, so a later p-move (case
    30) fast-path reuses a rawLL built at the pre-slice scalar.

The two-line diff below routes the slice path through cpp_log_likelihood_marginal
under marginalK and invalidates the charLL cache before evaluating (so the
marginal evaluator does a full rebuild against the proposed scalar instead of the
p-only fast-path). The sampled-k path is byte-unchanged.

--- a/src/mcmc.cpp
+++ b/src/mcmc.cpp
@@ eval_slice_target (~3286)
   } else {
+    // (insert before the existing else)
+  } else if (data->marginalK) {
+    // MARGINAL-K-SLICE-001: slice target is the marginal-over-k likelihood.
+    // Invalidate charLL cache first so the marginal evaluator rebuilds against
+    // the proposed scalar rather than taking the p-only fast-path on stale rawLL.
+    state->charLLCacheReady = false;
+    logLik = cpp_log_likelihood_marginal(
+        *data, *state, state->parent, state->child, edgeLen,
+        state->rateLoss, state->rateLogSd, state->rateNeo, wsPtr);
+  } else {
     logLik = state->usePartitioned
       ? cpp_log_likelihood_partitioned(...)
       : cpp_log_likelihood(...);
   }

@@ slice_scalar_impl acceptance recompute (~3370)
       } else {
+        if (data->marginalK) {
+          state->charLLCacheReady = false;
+          state->logLik = cpp_log_likelihood_marginal(
+              *data, *state, state->parent, state->child, edgeLen,
+              state->rateLoss, state->rateLogSd, state->rateNeo, wsPtr);
+          state->partLogLik.clear();
+        } else {
         state->logLik = state->usePartitioned
           ? cpp_log_likelihood_partitioned(...)
           : cpp_log_likelihood(...);
         state->partLogLik.clear();
+        }
       }

Notes / correctness:
 - cpp_log_likelihood_marginal takes (McmcData&, McmcState&, parent, child,
   edgeLen, rateLoss, rateLogSd, rateNeo, ClWorkspace*) — same as the call already
   used in compute_full_loglik_at (mcmc.cpp:1004). It reads state->p for the
   per-character weights, which is what the marginal density requires.
 - Setting charLLCacheReady=false before the eval forces the !useCache rebuild
   branch (mcmc.cpp:4218), which honours the proposed edgeLen / rateLogSd and
   re-fills the cache. The eval also re-sets charLLCacheReady=true at line 4282,
   leaving a coherent cache for the next p-move fast-path. No risk to the p-move
   fast-path: the cache rebuilt here reflects the accepted scalar.
 - Alternative (simpler but slower) fix: route through compute_full_loglik_at
   (mcmc.cpp:994), which already dispatches on data.marginalK. That avoids
   duplicating the call signature but still needs the charLLCacheReady=false reset
   before each eval, since compute_full_loglik_at -> cpp_log_likelihood_marginal
   would otherwise hit the fast-path on stale rawLL.
